commit 4333ffbc2c80349968929c87d259b1f06c1c2307
Author:     Jonathan Saggau <jsaggau@enharmonichq.com>
AuthorDate: Mon Aug 31 12:16:30 2026 -0400
Commit:     Jonathan Saggau <jsaggau@enharmonichq.com>
CommitDate: Mon Aug 31 12:16:30 2026 -0400

    daemon hardening from the Phase 1 three-brand panel
    
    Routing. Dispatch-first, never forward on faith: a dispatch miss now
    refreshes that one upstream's catalogue once (covering calls that arrive
    before the first list) and refuses a name the upstream does not offer.
    The old prefix fallback would have bypassed Phase 2's deny list and kept
    Phase 3's renamed tools callable under their old names (codex leg). The
    bare-name passthrough survives only for the deployed env-var single
    mode, whose spec carries an explicitly empty prefix.
    
    Prefixes are honoured verbatim: the single-upstream override that forced
    '' made the daemon and validator disagree about the same file, and made
    adding a second server silently rename the first one's surface — the
    roadmap's own defect 2, measured still live by the panel.
    
    Listing. Upstreams are listed concurrently, without the upstream lock,
    on a session snapshot — one slow upstream withheld every later
    upstream's tools for its whole timeout, and a client listing during a
    600s build got nothing until it ended (claude leg, measured). The drain
    is bounded structurally: repeated cursor and 200-page ceiling (48,075
    pages accumulated in ten seconds from a cycling stub), under the short
    connect timeout rather than the build-sized one. Duplicate exposed names
    from a mid-drain mutation are dropped with a warning.
    
    Errors. An upstream's application-level JSON-RPC error is forwarded as
    the caller's error; it no longer tears down a healthy session and claims
    'not connected' (codex leg). known_broken now clears the public session
    reference immediately instead of waiting out a possibly-wedged teardown.
    The cursor refusal raises MCPError(INVALID_PARAMS) so both transport
    paths return -32602 with the message intact; '' counts as a cursor.
    
    Truthfulness. The config's env map now reaches the child (merged over
    the SDK's six-variable allowlist; the panel measured Drew's
    XCODEMCP_ALLOWED_FOLDERS never arriving). Instructions are derived from
    the config — a Python stub was being introduced to clients as Apple's
    Xcode bridge — and the multi text now says a missing upstream means
    re-list. Not-connected errors only mention Xcode and its dialog for a
    require_xcode upstream. The stall watchdog names both candidate waits
    instead of asserting the wrong one.
    
    Watched red: 9 failures against the pre-hardening daemon, including the
    degraded scenario wedged behind the cursor-cycling stub.

diff --git a/tools/tests/stub_mcp_server.py b/tools/tests/stub_mcp_server.py
index 6e97e55..fc757f0 100755
--- a/tools/tests/stub_mcp_server.py
+++ b/tools/tests/stub_mcp_server.py
@@ -10,7 +10,11 @@ argument so any test (or any other agent's harness) can shape it without editing
     stub_mcp_server.py --name chatty --banner "starting"  # spec-illegal stdout banner line
     stub_mcp_server.py --name pager --tool a=1 --tool b=2 --page-size 1   # paginates
 
---tool NAME=REPLY declares one tool; calling it returns REPLY as text content.
+--tool NAME=REPLY declares one tool; calling it returns REPLY as text content. Two REPLY
+  prefixes change the behaviour: "env:VAR" answers with os.environ[VAR] resolved at call
+  time (proves an env pass-through by effect), and "error:MSG" answers with a JSON-RPC
+  ERROR response (an application-level error a proxy must forward, not treat as a dead
+  transport).
 --banner LINE prints LINE to stdout before serving (reproduces the class of server that
   breaks naive next-line readers).
 --notify-before-reply emits a spec-legal notification line immediately before every
@@ -19,10 +23,13 @@ argument so any test (or any other agent's harness) can shape it without editing
   the approval-dialog gate that must read as a timeout, never as an empty tool list).
 --page-size N makes tools/list return N tools per page with a nextCursor, exercising
   per-upstream cursors.
+--page-loop makes every tools/list page carry nextCursor "0" forever (a cursor cycle,
+  the upstream bug a drain must bound rather than follow to memory exhaustion).
 Python 3.9-compatible; no third-party imports.
 """
 import argparse
 import json
+import os
 import sys
 
 
@@ -35,6 +42,7 @@ def main():
     ap.add_argument("--notify-before-reply", action="store_true")
     ap.add_argument("--stall-tools", action="store_true")
     ap.add_argument("--page-size", type=int, default=0)
+    ap.add_argument("--page-loop", action="store_true")
     a = ap.parse_args()
 
     tools = {}
@@ -76,7 +84,10 @@ def main():
         elif method == "tools/list" and a.stall_tools:
             continue  # never answer — the approval-dialog gate, as a stub
         elif method == "tools/list":
-            if a.page_size > 0:
+            if a.page_loop:
+                send({"jsonrpc": "2.0", "id": mid, "result": {
+                    "tools": tool_list, "nextCursor": "0"}})
+            elif a.page_size > 0:
                 cursor = msg.get("params") or {}
                 start = int(cursor.get("cursor") or 0)
                 page = tool_list[start:start + a.page_size]
@@ -90,8 +101,15 @@ def main():
             params = msg.get("params") or {}
             name = params.get("name")
             if name in tools:
+                reply = tools[name]
+                if reply.startswith("env:"):
+                    reply = os.environ.get(reply[4:], "<unset:%s>" % reply[4:])
+                if reply.startswith("error:"):
+                    send({"jsonrpc": "2.0", "id": mid, "error": {
+                        "code": -32050, "message": reply[6:]}})
+                    continue
                 send({"jsonrpc": "2.0", "id": mid, "result": {
-                    "content": [{"type": "text", "text": tools[name]}], "isError": False}})
+                    "content": [{"type": "text", "text": reply}], "isError": False}})
             else:
                 send({"jsonrpc": "2.0", "id": mid, "error": {
                     "code": -32602, "message": "unknown tool %r on stub %s" % (name, a.name)}})
