     6	
     7	    relay and deployment hardening from the Phase 5 three-brand panel
     8	    
     9	    The relay. A break detected on the CALL path now fires the
    10	    surface-change notification — the dedicated _mark_broken was dead code
    11	    and the fastest break-detection path was the one that stayed silent, so
    12	    clients kept a cached full list through the whole outage (claude leg;
    13	    the fire happens outside the upstream lock). Modern-protocol
    14	    (2026-07-28+) clients were told listChanged:false because the SDK
    15	    derives that era's capability from a subscriptions/listen handler, not
    16	    NotificationOptions (codex leg, probed both paths): the daemon now
    17	    serves ListenHandler over an InMemorySubscriptionBus and publishes
    18	    ToolsListChanged beside the legacy per-session pushes. The downstream
    19	    registry is bounded at 128 with insertion-order eviction — an abandoned
    20	    legacy session's send neither raises nor drains, so failure-based
    21	    eviction never fired and the registry grew per client forever (all
    22	    three brands) — and broadcasts run concurrently with one timeout each.
    23	    
    24	    Versions. An upstream that omits serverInfo.version no longer passes
    25	    the check silently (found is reported as '(unreported)'); an upstream
    26	    back on its expected version retracts the advisory instead of telling
    27	    every new session about a mismatch that healed; a malformed mismatch
    28	    ledger degrades to warning twice instead of failing every reconnect
    29	    with a TypeError.
    30	    
    31	    Deployment. run.sh validates the config before ANY side effect — the
    32	    unservable placeholder still dies loudly, but no longer preempts
    33	    processes, rewrites .mcp.json to a dead endpoint, and leaves a port
    34	    file first. Self-preempt is pid-file-plus-argv-verified instead of a
    35	    pgrep -f pattern, which is an unanchored regex the panel demonstrated
    36	    matching a bystander tail -f on the installed file; TERM escalates to
    37	    KILL after a bounded wait, covering the wedged-teardown state the
    38	    daemon's own watchdog documents. A jq failure on .mcp.json kills the
    39	    launch where launchd can see it. The cold-install lesson from the
    40	    drews incident finally lands where the phase-1 synthesis said it
    41	    belonged: a 60-second default connect timeout for repo daemons. The
    42	    installer runs under set -e and accepts git worktrees (a worktree's
    43	    .git is a file, and the checkout set here includes one).
    44	    
    45	    Tests. The KeepAlive assertion reads the key via PlistBuddy — the
    46	    substring check was satisfied by RunAtLoad's <true/> with KeepAlive
    47	    absent, a genuine tautology. The relaunch section removes run 1's port
    48	    file and asserts the old pid actually died, so it can no longer pass
    49	    against a run.sh that does nothing; a live bystander tail -f must
    50	    survive the preempt; the trap's variables are initialised so an early
    51	    exit cannot error the trap itself and leak daemons.
    52	
